{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1789884720530,"sessionID":"ses_f428ff92affeQ6wiY0s4pQPZG0","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_d4f04bf94e62463f9a50e66f","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"C:\\xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\data\\exams\\rkq7bk65si88kk\\source.txt","offset":742},"output":"<path>C:\\xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\data\\exams\\rkq7bk65si88kk\\source.txt</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n742: Moderate / Faster than\n743: Incremental\n744: Moderate\n745: 4. Mirror Backup\n746: Creates an exact,\n747: real-time copy of source\n748: data, continuously\n749: updated. Does not keep\n750: older versions of files\n751: (no version history). If\n752: a file is deleted from\n753: source, it is removed\n754: from mirror (no rollback\n755: protection).\n756: Real-time\n757: Fastest Access\n758: High Storage / No\n759: Versioning\n760: B. Preparation (Incident Handling Phase 1)\n761: • Overview: Preparation is the first phase of Incident Handling. Goal: Get the organization ready before an incident happens.\n762: Reduces damage and response time. Involves people, processes, and technology readiness.\n763: • Policies & Plans:\n764: • Establish an Incident Response Policy.\n765: • Develop an Incident Response Plan (IRP).\n766: • Define roles and responsibilities (Incident Response Team).\n767: • Set up communication and escalation procedures.\n768: • Tools & Resources:\n769: • Deploy security tools (IDS/IPS, firewalls, antivirus, logging systems).\n770: • Maintain updated hardware/software inventory.\n771: • Prepare forensic tools and \"jump kit\" (forensic drives, cables, checklists).\n772: \n773: --- PAGE 18 ---\n774: IAS 2 Reviewer | Information Assurance and Security 2 (ITAS311-T)\n775: Page 18 of 20\n776: • Ensure backup systems are in place and tested.\n777: • Training & Testing:\n778: • Ongoing cycle: regular employee awareness training, tabletop drills/simulations, testing communication channels, periodic plan\n779: review.\n780: C. Identification (Incident Handling Phase 2)\n781: • Overview: Separates true security incidents from false alarms, determining scope, nature, and severity. Early, accurate\n782: identification limits damage.\n783: • Detection Sources: Alerts from IDS/IPS, antivirus, SIEM tools; log analysis; employee reports; system/network performance\n784: anomalies.\n785: • Verification Steps:\n786: • Confirm the event is a real security incident.\n787: • Gather initial evidence (timestamps, affected systems, indicators).\n788: • Classify incident type and severity level.\n789: • Document findings as they're discovered (chain of custody).\n790: • Next Actions: Notify Incident Response Team, assign handler/owner, preserve evidence, move to Containment once confirmed.\n791: D. The Three-Phase Response Model\n792: Once an incident is detected, three coordinated phases determine how well an organization survives: $$\\text{Notification} \\longrightarrow\n793: \\text{Response} \\longrightarrow \\text{Recovery}$$\n794: D1. Notification\n795: • Alert the right people, at the right time, through the right channels. Notification lists must be prepared before an incident\n796: occurs.\n797: • Who Should Be Informed (5 Groups): 1. Internal IT & Security Teams: Incident response team, sysadmins, network/security\n798: staff who investigate and act. 2. Management & Executives: Leadership needing situational awareness and approving major\n799: decisions. 3. Legal & Compliance Officers: Assess regulatory obligations, liability exposure, and mandatory disclosure. 4. Affected\n800: Users & Customers: Anyone whose data, accounts, or services were impacted, so they can protect themselves. 5. Law\n801: Enforcement & Regulators: Required for certain incidents (breaches, fraud) under law or regulation.\n802: • Reporting the Incident (4 Principles):\n803: • Standardized Reports: Detail what happened, when, affected systems, initial impact.\n804: • Defined Communication: Follow pre-defined chains to reach people first time.\n805: • Audit Trail Logging: Log every single step with precise timestamps and actions.\n806: • Factual Accuracy: Strictly factual and updated; avoid speculation.\n807: • Why Timely Notification Matters (4 Reasons): 1. Limits the Damage (early isolation stops spread). 2. Meets Legal Deadlines\n808: (strict windows, e.g., 72 hours). 3. Preserves Vital Trust (prompt, honest disclosure). 4. Speeds Up Recovery (faster stakeholder\n809: engagement).\n810: D2. Response (Containment & Eradication)\n811: • Containing the Incident: Stops spread while preserving evidence:\n812: • Isolate affected systems: Disconnect devices or network segments to stop lateral movement.\n813: • Disable compromised accounts: Suspend or reset credentials.\n814: • Apply short-term fixes: Block malicious IPs, close exploited ports, apply emergency firewall rules.\n815: • Preserve evidence: Capture logs, memory dumps, and system states before making changes.\n816: • Preventing Further Damage (4 Steps): 1. Continuous Monitoring (signs of spreading or recurring). 2. Patch Vulnerabilities (patch\n817: related flaws elsewhere). 3. Segment Networks (limit future blast radius). 4. Staff Communication (interim safety measures).\n818: • Removing the Cause (Eradication - 4 Steps): 1. Root Cause Identification (malware, misconfiguration, credentials, unpatched\n819: flaw). 2. Malware Eradication (eradicate malicious code, backdoors, unauthorized accounts). 3. Close Vulnerabilities (close specific\n820: vulnerability that allowed the incident). 4. Eradication Verification (verify eradication with scans and audits before declaring clean).\n821: D3. Recovery\n822: \n823: --- PAGE 19 ---\n824: IAS 2 Reviewer | Information Assurance and Security 2 (ITAS311-T)\n825: Page 19 of 20\n826: • Restoring Systems & Data From Backups: Brings business back online using clean, verified backups — never data that may\n827: still be compromised.\n828: • Identify a Clean Restore Point: Backup taken before incident occurred, confirmed free of malware.\n829: • Rebuild on Trusted Media: Reinstall systems from verified, uninfected sources.\n830: • Restore in Priority Order: Bring back critical business systems first.\n831: • Testing if Systems Work Properly (4 Steps): 1. Verify Functionality (confirm applications, services, data accurate and complete).\n832: 2. Security Scans (confirm no trace of original threat remains). 3. Performance Check (check performance under normal load). 4.\n833: Obtain Sign-Off (get sign-off from IT, security, and business owners before going live).\n834: • Returning to Normal Operations (4 Steps): 1. Gradual Rollout (bring systems online gradually, monitor closely). 2. Notify\n835: Stakeholders (inform users services are restored and safe). 3. Resume Monitoring (resume normal monitoring routines). 4. Lessons\n836: Learned (document lessons learned and update IR plan).\n837: E. Post Incident Review (PIR)\n838: • 4 Key Questions: 1. What happened, and when? 2. Were the documented procedures followed, and were they adequate? 3.\n839: What slowed the recovery down? 4. What do we do differently next time?\n840: • Find the Real Cause, Not Just the Symptom:\n841: • Symptom: \"The server went down.\"\n842: • Real Cause: Nobody was watching the disk space, and nobody had ever tested a restore.\n843: • Method: Keep asking \"why?\" until reaching something you can actually fix $\\rightarrow$ The 5 Whys.\n844: • Check If the Backup Really Worked (Two Questions): 1. How much data did we lose, and could we afford to lose that much?\n845: (This is the RPO). 2. How long were we down, and was that fast enough? (This is the RTO).\n846: • A backup that turns out damaged or three weeks old is itself a finding.\n847: • Every finding needs a person in charge and a due date. Then update response plan, backup policy, and guides.\n848: • Numbers to Track:\n849: • MTTD: How long it took to notice the problem (Mean Time to Detect).\n850: • MTTR: How long it took to fix it (Mean Time to Remediate / Resolve).\n851: • Total downtime, data lost, and cost.\n852: F. Documentation\n853: • Starts the moment you detect the incident — you do NOT write it at the end.\n854: • The Running Log: Responders write down each step, with the time, while they are still working on the problem.\n855: • What to Write Down (8 Key Items): 1. Incident ID: One number that connects all records together. 2. Discovery: When it was\n856: found, how it was found, and who found it. 3. Scope: Which systems, applications, and data were affected. 4. Severity: How serious\n857: it was rated, and who rated it. 5. Actions: Every step taken, in order, with the exact time. 6. Notifications: Who was told, when, and\n858: through what channel. 7. Evidence: Copies of logs, file checksums, and record of who handled them. 8. Backup Used: Which\n859: backup was restored, its date, and whether it was tested.\n860: • Golden Rule: Write facts only. Never write guesses.\n861: • Legal Status: The log is evidence. It can be used in court, in an insurance claim, or in a government audit. Keep access\n862: restricted, and keep the log for as long as policy requires.\n863: G. Reporting\n864: • One Incident, Five Different Readers: 1. Technical Team: Full timeline, signs of attack, real cause, and fixes still to be done. 2.\n865: Management: Short summary of 1 to 2 pages (cost, downtime, business impact, resources needed to prevent recurrence). 3. Legal\n866: Team and Government Regulator: Official breach notice filed before the legal deadline. 4. Affected Users: Plain language (what\n867: happened, what personal data was exposed, what they should do now). 5. Law Enforcement: Only when a crime was committed\n868: (fraud, extortion, theft of data).\n869: • What Goes Inside the Written Report (10 Parts): 1. Executive Summary: Written last, read first. 2. Incident Overview: What it \n870: was, and how serious. 3. Timeline: From detection to closing, with times. 4. Systems and Data Affected: What was hit, and how \n871: much. 5. Root Cause: The real reason, not the symptom. 6. Response and Recovery: What the team did, including which backup \n872: was used. 7. Impact Assessment: The section managers read twice (justifies budget for fixes; money lost, downtime, data lost,\n873: \n874: --- PAGE 20 ---\n875: IAS 2 Reviewer | Information Assurance and Security 2 (ITAS311-T)\n876: Page 20 of 20\n877: reputation damage). 8. Lessons Learned: What worked, and what did not. 9. Recommendations: Each one with a person in charge\n878: and a due date. 10. Appendices: Logs, screenshots, and other evidence.\n879: Required by Law (Republic Act 10173 & NPC Circular 16-03)\n880: • Republic Act 10173 (Data Privacy Act of 2012) & NPC Circular 16-03:\n881: • 72 Hours: Notify the National Privacy Commission (NPC) and the people whose data was exposed. The clock starts when you\n882: know, or have good reason to believe, that a breach happened. It does not start when your investigation is finished.\n883: • 5 Days: Submit the full breach report, counted from the day you discovered the breach. Asking the NPC for an exemption does not\n884: stop the clock.\n885: • Notice Requirements: Must state what happened, what personal data was involved, what was done to reduce harm, and who to\n886: contact (such as the Data Protection Officer).\n887: • Enforcement Example: H&M Philippines was ordered by the NPC to explain why it missed the 72-hour deadline after a payment\n888: card incident in 2018.\n889: H. Summary: Key Takeaways\n890: • Follow-up turns an outage into an improvement: Find the real cause, not the symptom. Give every fix a person in charge and a\n891: due date. Confirm the backup met the RPO and RTO.\n892: • Documentation is written during the incident, not after it: It is a running log of facts, with times. It counts as evidence, so keep\n893: it factual and keep access restricted.\n894: • Reporting changes with the reader: Five audiences, five levels of detail. When personal data is involved, the deadline is 72\n895: hours by law.\n\n(End of file - total 895 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"Moderate / Faster than\nIncremental\nModerate\n4. Mirror Backup\nCreates an exact,\nreal-time copy of source\ndata, continuously\nupdated. Does not keep\nolder versions of files\n(no version history). If\na file is deleted from\nsource, it is removed\nfrom mirror (no rollback\nprotection).\nReal-time\nFastest Access\nHigh Storage / No\nVersioning\nB. Preparation (Incident Handling Phase 1)\n• Overview: Preparation is the first phase of Incident Handling. Goal: Get the organization ready before an incident happens.","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"C:\\xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\data\\exams\\rkq7bk65si88kk\\source.txt","text":"Moderate / Faster than\nIncremental\nModerate\n4. Mirror Backup\nCreates an exact,\nreal-time copy of source\ndata, continuously\nupdated. Does not keep\nolder versions of files\n(no version history). If\na file is deleted from\nsource, it is removed\nfrom mirror (no rollback\nprotection).\nReal-time\nFastest Access\nHigh Storage / No\nVersioning\nB. Preparation (Incident Handling Phase 1)\n• Overview: Preparation is the first phase of Incident Handling. Goal: Get the organization ready before an incident happens.\nReduces damage and response time. Involves people, processes, and technology readiness.\n• Policies & Plans:\n• Establish an Incident Response Policy.\n• Develop an Incident Response Plan (IRP).\n• Define roles and responsibilities (Incident Response Team).\n• Set up communication and escalation procedures.\n• Tools & Resources:\n• Deploy security tools (IDS/IPS, firewalls, antivirus, logging systems).\n• Maintain updated hardware/software inventory.\n• Prepare forensic tools and \"jump kit\" (forensic drives, cables, checklists).\n\n--- PAGE 18 ---\nIAS 2 Reviewer | Information Assurance and Security 2 (ITAS311-T)\nPage 18 of 20\n• Ensure backup systems are in place and tested.\n• Training & Testing:\n• Ongoing cycle: regular employee awareness training, tabletop drills/simulations, testing communication channels, periodic plan\nreview.\nC. Identification (Incident Handling Phase 2)\n• Overview: Separates true security incidents from false alarms, determining scope, nature, and severity. Early, accurate\nidentification limits damage.\n• Detection Sources: Alerts from IDS/IPS, antivirus, SIEM tools; log analysis; employee reports; system/network performance\nanomalies.\n• Verification Steps:\n• Confirm the event is a real security incident.\n• Gather initial evidence (timestamps, affected systems, indicators).\n• Classify incident type and severity level.\n• Document findings as they're discovered (chain of custody).\n• Next Actions: Notify Incident Response Team, assign handler/owner, preserve evidence, move to Containment once confirmed.\nD. The Three-Phase Response Model\nOnce an incident is detected, three coordinated phases determine how well an organization survives: $$\\text{Notification} \\longrightarrow\n\\text{Response} \\longrightarrow \\text{Recovery}$$\nD1. Notification\n• Alert the right people, at the right time, through the right channels. Notification lists must be prepared before an incident\noccurs.\n• Who Should Be Informed (5 Groups): 1. Internal IT & Security Teams: Incident response team, sysadmins, network/security\nstaff who investigate and act. 2. Management & Executives: Leadership needing situational awareness and approving major\ndecisions. 3. Legal & Compliance Officers: Assess regulatory obligations, liability exposure, and mandatory disclosure. 4. Affected\nUsers & Customers: Anyone whose data, accounts, or services were impacted, so they can protect themselves. 5. Law\nEnforcement & Regulators: Required for certain incidents (breaches, fraud) under law or regulation.\n• Reporting the Incident (4 Principles):\n• Standardized Reports: Detail what happened, when, affected systems, initial impact.\n• Defined Communication: Follow pre-defined chains to reach people first time.\n• Audit Trail Logging: Log every single step with precise timestamps and actions.\n• Factual Accuracy: Strictly factual and updated; avoid speculation.\n• Why Timely Notification Matters (4 Reasons): 1. Limits the Damage (early isolation stops spread). 2. Meets Legal Deadlines\n(strict windows, e.g., 72 hours). 3. Preserves Vital Trust (prompt, honest disclosure). 4. Speeds Up Recovery (faster stakeholder\nengagement).\nD2. Response (Containment & Eradication)\n• Containing the Incident: Stops spread while preserving evidence:\n• Isolate affected systems: Disconnect devices or network segments to stop lateral movement.\n• Disable compromised accounts: Suspend or reset credentials.\n• Apply short-term fixes: Block malicious IPs, close exploited ports, apply emergency firewall rules.\n• Preserve evidence: Capture logs, memory dumps, and system states before making changes.\n• Preventing Further Damage (4 Steps): 1. Continuous Monitoring (signs of spreading or recurring). 2. Patch Vulnerabilities (patch\nrelated flaws elsewhere). 3. Segment Networks (limit future blast radius). 4. Staff Communication (interim safety measures).\n• Removing the Cause (Eradication - 4 Steps): 1. Root Cause Identification (malware, misconfiguration, credentials, unpatched\nflaw). 2. Malware Eradication (eradicate malicious code, backdoors, unauthorized accounts). 3. Close Vulnerabilities (close specific\nvulnerability that allowed the incident). 4. Eradication Verification (verify eradication with scans and audits before declaring clean).\nD3. Recovery\n\n--- PAGE 19 ---\nIAS 2 Reviewer | Information Assurance and Security 2 (ITAS311-T)\nPage 19 of 20\n• Restoring Systems & Data From Backups: Brings business back online using clean, verified backups — never data that may\nstill be compromised.\n• Identify a Clean Restore Point: Backup taken before incident occurred, confirmed free of malware.\n• Rebuild on Trusted Media: Reinstall systems from verified, uninfected sources.\n• Restore in Priority Order: Bring back critical business systems first.\n• Testing if Systems Work Properly (4 Steps): 1. Verify Functionality (confirm applications, services, data accurate and complete).\n2. Security Scans (confirm no trace of original threat remains). 3. Performance Check (check performance under normal load). 4.\nObtain Sign-Off (get sign-off from IT, security, and business owners before going live).\n• Returning to Normal Operations (4 Steps): 1. Gradual Rollout (bring systems online gradually, monitor closely). 2. Notify\nStakeholders (inform users services are restored and safe). 3. Resume Monitoring (resume normal monitoring routines). 4. Lessons\nLearned (document lessons learned and update IR plan).\nE. Post Incident Review (PIR)\n• 4 Key Questions: 1. What happened, and when? 2. Were the documented procedures followed, and were they adequate? 3.\nWhat slowed the recovery down? 4. What do we do differently next time?\n• Find the Real Cause, Not Just the Symptom:\n• Symptom: \"The server went down.\"\n• Real Cause: Nobody was watching the disk space, and nobody had ever tested a restore.\n• Method: Keep asking \"why?\" until reaching something you can actually fix $\\rightarrow$ The 5 Whys.\n• Check If the Backup Really Worked (Two Questions): 1. How much data did we lose, and could we afford to lose that much?\n(This is the RPO). 2. How long were we down, and was that fast enough? (This is the RTO).\n• A backup that turns out damaged or three weeks old is itself a finding.\n• Every finding needs a person in charge and a due date. Then update response plan, backup policy, and guides.\n• Numbers to Track:\n• MTTD: How long it took to notice the problem (Mean Time to Detect).\n• MTTR: How long it took to fix it (Mean Time to Remediate / Resolve).\n• Total downtime, data lost, and cost.\nF. Documentation\n• Starts the moment you detect the incident — you do NOT write it at the end.\n• The Running Log: Responders write down each step, with the time, while they are still working on the problem.\n• What to Write Down (8 Key Items): 1. Incident ID: One number that connects all records together. 2. Discovery: When it was\nfound, how it was found, and who found it. 3. Scope: Which systems, applications, and data were affected. 4. Severity: How serious\nit was rated, and who rated it. 5. Actions: Every step taken, in order, with the exact time. 6. Notifications: Who was told, when, and\nthrough what channel. 7. Evidence: Copies of logs, file checksums, and record of who handled them. 8. Backup Used: Which\nbackup was restored, its date, and whether it was tested.\n• Golden Rule: Write facts only. Never write guesses.\n• Legal Status: The log is evidence. It can be used in court, in an insurance claim, or in a government audit. Keep access\nrestricted, and keep the log for as long as policy requires.\nG. Reporting\n• One Incident, Five Different Readers: 1. Technical Team: Full timeline, signs of attack, real cause, and fixes still to be done. 2.\nManagement: Short summary of 1 to 2 pages (cost, downtime, business impact, resources needed to prevent recurrence). 3. Legal\nTeam and Government Regulator: Official breach notice filed before the legal deadline. 4. Affected Users: Plain language (what\nhappened, what personal data was exposed, what they should do now). 5. Law Enforcement: Only when a crime was committed\n(fraud, extortion, theft of data).\n• What Goes Inside the Written Report (10 Parts): 1. Executive Summary: Written last, read first. 2. Incident Overview: What it \nwas, and how serious. 3. Timeline: From detection to closing, with times. 4. Systems and Data Affected: What was hit, and how \nmuch. 5. Root Cause: The real reason, not the symptom. 6. Response and Recovery: What the team did, including which backup \nwas used. 7. Impact Assessment: The section managers read twice (justifies budget for fixes; money lost, downtime, data lost,\n\n--- PAGE 20 ---\nIAS 2 Reviewer | Information Assurance and Security 2 (ITAS311-T)\nPage 20 of 20\nreputation damage). 8. Lessons Learned: What worked, and what did not. 9. Recommendations: Each one with a person in charge\nand a due date. 10. Appendices: Logs, screenshots, and other evidence.\nRequired by Law (Republic Act 10173 & NPC Circular 16-03)\n• Republic Act 10173 (Data Privacy Act of 2012) & NPC Circular 16-03:\n• 72 Hours: Notify the National Privacy Commission (NPC) and the people whose data was exposed. The clock starts when you\nknow, or have good reason to believe, that a breach happened. It does not start when your investigation is finished.\n• 5 Days: Submit the full breach report, counted from the day you discovered the breach. Asking the NPC for an exemption does not\nstop the clock.\n• Notice Requirements: Must state what happened, what personal data was involved, what was done to reduce harm, and who to\ncontact (such as the Data Protection Officer).\n• Enforcement Example: H&M Philippines was ordered by the NPC to explain why it missed the 72-hour deadline after a payment\ncard incident in 2018.\nH. Summary: Key Takeaways\n• Follow-up turns an outage into an improvement: Find the real cause, not the symptom. Give every fix a person in charge and a\ndue date. Confirm the backup met the RPO and RTO.\n• Documentation is written during the incident, not after it: It is a running log of facts, with times. It counts as evidence, so keep\nit factual and keep access restricted.\n• Reporting changes with the reader: Five audiences, five levels of detail. When personal data is involved, the deadline is 72\nhours by law.","lineStart":742,"lineEnd":895,"totalLines":895,"truncated":false}},"title":"xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\data\\exams\\rkq7bk65si88kk\\source.txt","time":{"start":1789884720517,"end":1789884720527}},"id":"prt_0bd7164ad001M3PLwScMjB6JAY","sessionID":"ses_f428ff92affeQ6wiY0s4pQPZG0","messageID":"msg_0bd715b5d001F1N5X4ub1t5ush"}}
