{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1789885156806,"sessionID":"ses_f428ff92affeQ6wiY0s4pQPZG0","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"task","callID":"call_16e8e495461e40f6ae25fc47","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"description":"Generate ch4 question bank","prompt":"You are building CHAPTER 4 of a 6-chapter interactive exam system. You are doing RESEARCH AND AUTHORING only — your ONLY deliverable is two JSON files written to disk with the Write tool.\n\nSOURCE OF TRUTH (read it with your Read tool before writing anything):\nC:\\xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\data\\exams\\rkq7bk65si88kk\\source.txt\nIt is a plain-text extraction of an \"IAS 2 Information Assurance & Security 2\" reviewer PDF. Chapter 4 corresponds to LESSON L6 \"Risk Management Process\", which lives on the pages marked \"PAGE 11\", \"PAGE 12\", and \"PAGE 13\" of that file (approx lines 437-551). Focus on those pages. Everything you write MUST be grounded in that text. NEVER invent facts. If a concept is too thin to support a question, do not force it.\n\nOUTPUT FILES (absolute paths; the parent folder already exists):\n1) C:\\Users\\kelly\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\opencode\\ias2build\\ch4.json\n   Content: a JSON ARRAY containing exactly ONE chapter object:\n   [\n     {\n       \"chapterId\": \"ch4\",\n       \"title\": \"L6: Risk Management Process\",\n       \"description\": \"This chapter covers identifying, assessing, and responding to risks, including qualitative vs quantitative assessment, the risk matrix, and the four response strategies.\",\n       \"questions\": [\n         {\n           \"id\": \"ch4-q01\",\n           \"question\": \"...\",\n           \"choices\": [\"...\", \"...\", \"...\", \"...\"],\n           \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n           \"explanation\": \"...\"\n         }\n       ]\n     }\n   ]\n   The array must be valid JSON. Use double quotes, escape internal quotes, no trailing commas.\n\n2) C:\\Users\\kelly\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\opencode\\ias2build\\enc4.json\n   Content: a JSON ARRAY of 10-12 encyclopedia entries for this chapter:\n   [\n     { \"chapterId\": \"ch4\", \"term\": \"Risk Register\", \"definition\": \"Definition grounded in the reference.\" }\n   ]\n\n=== REQUIRED COVERAGE ===\nProduce EXACTLY 30 questions, each drawing from a different concept below (each concept exactly once; do NOT ask the same fact twice with different wording). Cover at minimum the concepts marked with *:\n1* Risk identification definition (finding, recognizing, documenting potential risks)\n2* Risk identification is the STARTING POINT of the risk management process + core guiding question (\"what could go wrong while developing our system?\")\n3* The 5 reasons risk identification is important\n4* Brainstorming technique (team members identify risks like lack of resources, technical errors, delays)\n5* Checklist technique (list of common risks from previous projects or standards)\n6* SWOT analysis (Strengths, Weaknesses, Opportunities, Threats)\n7* Expert interviews technique (cybersecurity expert may identify vulnerabilities the team overlooks)\n8* Documentation review technique (project documents, contracts, schedules, requirements, reports)\n9* Historical information technique (risks from previous or similar projects)\n10* Risk register: definition (document recording identified risks) + the identification process (identify -> document -> record -> proceed to risk assessment)\n11* Risk assessment definition (identifying, analyzing, evaluating risks to determine significance)\n12* Qualitative risk assessment: basis/rating (descriptive judgment-based Low/Medium/High), speed & effort (fast, easy to communicate, quick results), data requirement (works with limited historical data) - including the startup-vendor \"Medium\" example\n13* Quantitative risk assessment: basis (numerical/data-driven dollar amounts, probabilities), speed (takes longer, demands detailed historical data), tools (statistical analysis tools, specific numerical outcomes) - including the bank $2.3M expected annual loss example\n14* Comparative question distinguishing qualitative from quantitative (a bank computes a $2.3M expected annual loss - which approach?)\n15* Likelihood definition (probability a risk event will occur)\n16* Impact definition (severity of consequences if the risk event occurs)\n17* Fundamental formula: Risk Level = Likelihood x Impact (include a computation scenario, e.g., likelihood 4, impact 3 -> level 12)\n18* Severity ranking: high-severity -> immediate mitigation/action; low-severity -> monitor or accept; ensures efficient resource allocation\n19* The risk matrix (visual grid plotting likelihood Y-axis vs impact X-axis; color-coded green/yellow/red cells)\n20* The 4 steps of risk management (identify, assess, implement the risk response plan, monitor and control) - sequence\n21* Implementing the risk response plan definition + what it involves (resource allocation, assigning responsibilities, setting timelines, selecting the right tools)\n22* Risk response strategy: Avoid (eliminate the risk entirely) - with an application scenario\n23* Risk response strategy: Mitigate (reduce the likelihood or impact) - with an application scenario\n24* Risk response strategy: Transfer (shift risk to a third party) - with an application scenario\n25* Risk response strategy: Accept (acknowledge and monitor without action) - with an application scenario\n26* The implementation process 6 steps (finalize strategy, assign roles & responsibilities, allocate budget & resources, set timeline/schedule, execute planned actions, document the implementation) - sequence\n27* Successful implementation requires (clear ownership, adequate resources, open communication)\n28* Monitor and control risk response definition (ongoing process of tracking identified risks, reviewing effectiveness, identifying new risks) + involves (tracking, reviewing, adjusting, reporting)\n29* Monitoring techniques and tools (regular risk audits & inspections, KRIs and dashboards, status reports & team check-ins, feedback loops from stakeholders)\n30* Likelihood and impact are usually rated on a scale (e.g., 1-5 or Low/Med/High)\n\n=== QUESTION QUALITY RULES (HARD) ===\n- Exactly 4 choices per question, exactly ONE correct answer (index 0=A,1=B,2=C,3=D). No \"all of the above\", no \"none of the above\", no two-correct answers, nothing a knowledgeable student could dispute.\n- Choice parity: all four choices must be similar in length (word/character count), same level of specificity, technicality, grammar, and professional tone. The correct answer must NEVER be the longest, most detailed, most specific, or most technical option. Rotate which position carries the longer text so length is not a clue.\n- No keyword leakage: do not repeat a distinctive phrase from the question inside the correct choice unless the distractors contain it too.\n- Distractors must be plausible: use nearby concepts (other techniques, other strategies, qualitative vs quantitative traits) or common misconceptions. Never absurd distractors.\n- Avoid absolute words (always/never/only/completely/guaranteed) unless semantically justified and balanced across options.\n- Question wording: plain and direct. Vary cognitive level: ~35% recall/understanding, 35% application, 25% analysis, 5% evaluation. Use scenarios (buying insurance = transfer; patching = mitigate; canceling the project = avoid), comparisons, exceptions (\"Which is NOT...\"), sequences, and a math/formula question. Mark negative wording clearly.\n- Every question needs a 1-3 sentence explanation stating why the correct answer is right and briefly why the others are not, grounded in the reference.\n- ANSWER DISTRIBUTION (HARD): your 30 correctAnswers must be exactly A=7, B=7, C=8, D=8 (0=A,1=B,2=C,3=D). No letter appears more than 2 consecutive questions. No cycling pattern like A,B,C,D,A,B,C,D.\n- Check JSON validity before finishing.\n\n=== ESCAPING NOTE ===\nStrings are plain UTF-8 text; prefer ASCII-friendly phrasing (write \"x\" or \"multiplied by\" instead of symbol). Escape internal double quotes with \\\". No backslashes otherwise.\n\n=== RETURN ===\nDo NOT print the JSON. In your final reply return only: (1) number of questions written, (2) exact correctAnswer letter distribution, (3) one-line confirmation that both files exist and are valid JSON, (4) any concept you had to drop and why.","subagent_type":"general"},"output":"<task id=\"ses_f428bb07effe4986QdayUGqqTJ\" state=\"completed\">\n<task_result>\nDone. Both files written and validated.\n\n1. **30 questions** written (ch4.json)\n2. **correctAnswer distribution:** A=7, B=7, C=8, D=8\n3. Both `ch4.json` and `enc4.json` exist and parse as valid JSON (verified with `ConvertFrom-Json`; 30 questions, 12 encyclopedia entries, all IDs unique).\n4. No concepts dropped — all 30 required concepts covered exactly once, including the 4-step and 6-step sequence questions, the math/formula question (q17), the NOT-style question (q21), and application scenarios for avoid/mitigate/transfer/accept.\n</task_result>\n</task>","metadata":{"parentSessionId":"ses_f428ff92affeQ6wiY0s4pQPZG0","sessionId":"ses_f428bb07effe4986QdayUGqqTJ","model":{"modelID":"big-pickle","providerID":"opencode"},"truncated":false},"title":"Generate ch4 question bank","time":{"start":1789884911493,"end":1789885156802}},"id":"prt_0bd742012001ZZR63MSEjtnas2","sessionID":"ses_f428ff92affeQ6wiY0s4pQPZG0","messageID":"msg_0bd7365d60013qmLhoE7nzwi0N"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1789885275505,"sessionID":"ses_f428ff92affeQ6wiY0s4pQPZG0","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"task","callID":"call_3543a4ad734d4a0ba60ce1e0","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"description":"Generate ch1 question bank","prompt":"You are building CHAPTER 1 of a 6-chapter interactive exam system. You are doing RESEARCH AND AUTHORING only — your ONLY deliverable is two JSON files written to disk with the Write tool.\n\nSOURCE OF TRUTH (read it with your Read tool before writing anything):\nC:\\xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\data\\exams\\rkq7bk65si88kk\\source.txt\nIt is a plain-text extraction of an \"IAS 2 Information Assurance & Security 2\" reviewer PDF. Chapter 1 corresponds to LESSON L3 \"Post Audit Activities\", which lives on the pages marked \"PAGE 2\", \"PAGE 3\", and \"PAGE 4\" of that file (approx lines 18-155). Focus on those pages. Everything you write MUST be grounded in that text. NEVER invent facts. If a concept is too thin to support a question, do not force it.\n\nOUTPUT FILES (absolute paths; the parent folder already exists):\n1) C:\\Users\\kelly\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\opencode\\ias2build\\ch1.json\n   Content: a JSON ARRAY containing exactly ONE chapter object, like so:\n   [\n     {\n       \"chapterId\": \"ch1\",\n       \"title\": \"L3: Post Audit Activities\",\n       \"description\": \"This chapter is a short intro that also covers the mechanics of the exit interview as a pre-report discussion meeting.\",\n       \"questions\": [\n         {\n           \"id\": \"ch1-q01\",\n           \"question\": \"...\",\n           \"choices\": [\"...\", \"...\", \"...\", \"...\"],\n           \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n           \"explanation\": \"...\"\n         }\n       ]\n     }\n   ]\n   The array must be valid JSON. Use double quotes, escape internal quotes, no trailing commas.\n\n2) C:\\Users\\kelly\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\opencode\\ias2build\\enc1.json\n   Content: a JSON ARRAY of 10-12 encyclopedia entries for this chapter:\n   [\n     { \"chapterId\": \"ch1\", \"term\": \"Exit Interview\", \"definition\": \"Definition grounded in the reference.\" }\n   ]\n\n=== REQUIRED COVERAGE ===\nProduce EXACTLY 30 questions, drawing each from a different concept below (select ~30 of the 33 concepts; each concept exactly once; do NOT ask the same fact twice with different wording). Cover at minimum the concepts marked with *:\n1* Definition of post-audit activities\n2* Core purpose of post-audit activities\n3* Exit interview definition + its alternate names (exit/closing meeting) + when it is held\n4* The three core objectives of the exit interview: Clarify, Discuss, Prepare\n5* Where the exit interview fits in the audit lifecycle (fieldwork -> exit interview -> management responses & comments -> draft report review -> final audit report)\n6* Role/purpose of the audit team (lead auditor) in the exit interview\n7* Role/purpose of management of the audited entity in the exit interview\n8* Role of \"other participants\" invited by the audit client\n9* Purpose: clarify findings / resolve misunderstandings\n10* Purpose: obtain management response\n11* Purpose: discuss improvements (recommendations, corrective actions, responsibilities, timelines)\n12* The two key questions auditors ask about missing information (WHO is responsible / WHEN can the auditor expect it) - use the IT access-control logs scenario\n13* What to prepare before the auditors leave (3 target dates: draft audit report, approval timeline, final report)\n14* Advantage: resolves issues early\n15* Advantage: prevents surprises\n16* Limitation: NOT required in every audit (held when necessary)\n17* Limitation: issues/disagreements may remain unresolved and are then documented in the audit report\n18* Limitation: communication gaps (technical findings hard for non-technical participants)\n19* Data analysis in auditing - definition\n20* Step 1: define questions and goals (targeted hypothesis vs open-ended questions)\n21* Step 2: collect data (sources, BI tool connections, volume vs questions, skewed results)\n22* Step 3: data wrangling (outliers, null values, incorrectly input data)\n23* Step 4: diagnostic analysis (cause of + solution to an existing problem)\n24* Step 4: descriptive analysis (summarizing key sections)\n25* Step 4: predictive analysis (historical data + statistical modeling to forecast)\n26* Step 5: interpret results (tell a story, prompt deeper questions, restart cycle)\n27* Audit report: purpose (formal communication, documentation of evidence, initiation of corrective action closure) and benefits\n28* The MOST CRITICAL content in an audit report is the Opinion\n29* Which standard format part confirms authenticity (Signature of the Auditor); also cover addressee or basis of opinion as a second part-based question\n30* Presentation of findings: definition + purposes (communicate results, ensure accountability, support decision-making, promote transparency)\n31 How findings are presented: organize by priority or risk level, not discovery order\n32 Step 3 of presenting: state finding, impact, fix\n33 What to prepare before the presentation (finalize report draft, anticipate questions, prioritize findings, set an agenda)\n\n=== QUESTION QUALITY RULES (HARD) ===\n- Exactly 4 choices per question, exactly ONE correct answer (index 0=A,1=B,2=C,3=D). No \"all of the above\", no \"none of the above\", no two-correct answers, nothing a knowledgeable student could dispute.\n- Choice parity: all four choices must be similar in length (word/character count), same level of specificity, technicality, grammar, and professional tone. The correct answer must NEVER be the longest, most detailed, most specific, or most technical option. Rotate which position carries the longer text so length is not a clue.\n- No keyword leakage: do not repeat a distinctive phrase from the question inside the correct choice unless the distractors contain it too.\n- Distractors must be plausible: use nearby concepts, common misconceptions, or correct concepts from the same lesson applied incorrectly. Never absurd distractors.\n- Avoid absolute words (always/never/only/completely/guaranteed) unless semantically justified and used as part of all-option parity.\n- Question wording: plain and direct. Vary the cognitive level: about 40% recall/understanding, 30% application, 20% analysis, 10% evaluation. Use short scenarios, comparisons, exceptions (\"Which is NOT...\"), and sequence questions. Mark negative wording clearly.\n- Every question needs a 1-3 sentence explanation stating why the correct answer is right and briefly why the others are not, grounded in the reference.\n- ANSWER DISTRIBUTION (HARD): your 30 correctAnswers must be exactly A=8, B=8, C=7, D=7 (0=A,1=B,2=C,3=D). No letter appears more than 2 consecutive questions. No cycling pattern like A,B,C,D,A,B,C,D.\n- Check JSON validity before finishing (e.g., mentally verify braces/quotes, or use a tool to validate).\n\n=== ESCAPING NOTE ===\nThe question/choice/explanation strings are plain text (UTF-8). Unusual math symbols are NOT needed; prefer ASCII-friendly phrasing. Escape double quotes inside strings with \\\". Do not include backslashes.\n\n=== RETURN ===\nDo NOT print the JSON. In your final reply return only: (1) number of questions written, (2) exact correctAnswer letter distribution, (3) one-line confirmation that both files exist and are valid JSON, (4) any concept you had to drop and why.","subagent_type":"general"},"output":"<task id=\"ses_f428c5de5ffe3w1gGFGGjg7t2U\" state=\"completed\">\n<task_result>\nBoth files written and validated.\n\n1. **30 questions** written (`ch1-q01` through `ch1-q30`).\n2. **Letter distribution exactly A=8, B=8, C=7, D=7** (max consecutive run = 2, no cycling pattern; verified programmatically: A=8, B=8, C=7, D=7).\n3. Both files exist and parsed as valid JSON: `ch1.json` (1 chapter, 30 questions) and `enc1.json` (12 entries).\n4. **Dropped concepts: 31, 32, 33** (How findings are presented / state finding-impact-fix / prepare before the presentation) — the only non-asterisked items, dropped to hit the exact 30-question target while covering all 30 required concepts. Note: concept 29's \"second part-based question\" note (addressee/basis of opinion) is folded into the explanation of ch1-q29, since all 30 slots were already consumed by the mandatory asterisked concepts.\n</task_result>\n</task>","metadata":{"parentSessionId":"ses_f428ff92affeQ6wiY0s4pQPZG0","sessionId":"ses_f428c5de5ffe3w1gGFGGjg7t2U","model":{"modelID":"big-pickle","providerID":"opencode"},"truncated":false},"title":"Generate ch1 question bank","time":{"start":1789884867102,"end":1789885275501}},"id":"prt_0bd736dc5001pVG45k6fn5yJnJ","sessionID":"ses_f428ff92affeQ6wiY0s4pQPZG0","messageID":"msg_0bd7365d60013qmLhoE7nzwi0N"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1789885457224,"sessionID":"ses_f428ff92affeQ6wiY0s4pQPZG0","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"task","callID":"call_fe7692f9f04642328e47be0d","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"description":"Generate ch5 question bank","prompt":"You are building CHAPTER 5 of a 6-chapter interactive exam system. You are doing RESEARCH AND AUTHORING only — your ONLY deliverable is two JSON files written to disk with the Write tool.\n\nSOURCE OF TRUTH (read it with your Read tool before writing anything):\nC:\\xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\data\\exams\\rkq7bk65si88kk\\source.txt\nIt is a plain-text extraction of an \"IAS 2 Information Assurance & Security 2\" reviewer PDF. Chapter 5 corresponds to LESSON L7 \"Business Continuity Management (BCM)\", which lives on the pages marked \"PAGE 14\", \"PAGE 15\", and \"PAGE 16\" of that file (approx lines 553-696). Focus on those pages. Everything you write MUST be grounded in that text. NEVER invent facts. If a concept is too thin to support a question, do not force it.\n\nOUTPUT FILES (absolute paths; the parent folder already exists):\n1) C:\\Users\\kelly\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\opencode\\ias2build\\ch5.json\n   Content: a JSON ARRAY containing exactly ONE chapter object:\n   [\n     {\n       \"chapterId\": \"ch5\",\n       \"title\": \"L7: Business Continuity Management (BCM)\",\n       \"description\": \"This chapter covers BCM/BCP/DR fundamentals, the recovery metrics RTO/RPO/MTD/WRT, Business Impact Analysis, and plan review and testing.\",\n       \"questions\": [\n         {\n           \"id\": \"ch5-q01\",\n           \"question\": \"...\",\n           \"choices\": [\"...\", \"...\", \"...\", \"...\"],\n           \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n           \"explanation\": \"...\"\n         }\n       ]\n     }\n   ]\n   The array must be valid JSON. Use double quotes, escape internal quotes, no trailing commas.\n\n2) C:\\Users\\kelly\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\opencode\\ias2build\\enc5.json\n   Content: a JSON ARRAY of 10-12 encyclopedia entries for this chapter:\n   [\n     { \"chapterId\": \"ch5\", \"term\": \"RTO\", \"definition\": \"Definition grounded in the reference.\" }\n   ]\n\n=== REQUIRED COVERAGE ===\nProduce EXACTLY 30 questions, each drawing from a different concept below (each concept exactly once; do NOT ask the same fact twice with different wording). Cover at minimum the concepts marked with *:\n1* BCM definition: organization-wide framework of policies, processes, and capabilities to prevent, respond to, and recover from disruptive incidents\n2* BCP definition: the documented set of procedures to keep critical functions running during and immediately after a disruption\n3* Disaster Recovery definition: a technical, IT-focused subset of continuity planning concerned with restoring systems, data, and infrastructure after an outage\n4* RTO: maximum acceptable time to restore a business function after a disruption\n5* RPO: maximum acceptable amount of data loss, measured as a point in time to which data must be restored\n6* MTD: the absolute limit of disruption a function can withstand before survival is threatened\n7* WRT: time needed after systems are restored to verify data and resume normal processing\n8* Incident vs Crisis vs Disaster (escalating levels of severity: minor disruption / threatens objectives & reputation / causes major often prolonged damage)\n9* Resilience: ability to absorb and adapt to disruption without losing function\n10* Single Point of Failure (SPOF): any single component whose failure stops the entire process\n11* Continuity of Operations: sustained delivery of critical services throughout a disruption, not merely eventual restoration\n12* MTD is also called Maximum Tolerable Period of Disruption (MTPD)\n13* MTD nature: not a target to aim for; the outer boundary every recovery strategy must stay inside\n14* What drives MTD (4): financial impact, reputational damage, regulatory & legal obligations, operational dependencies\n15* Cost escalation over time: impact accelerates (early hours absorbed by buffers/goodwill; compounds day by day until approaching the MTD ceiling)\n16* RPO/RTO/WRT fit within MTD: RPO looks backward to set tolerable data loss; RTO measures recovery effort; WRT covers verification before normal work resumes; together they must fit inside MTD\n17* The 5 steps to determine acceptable downtime (identify critical processes, consult stakeholders & regulators, quantify impact over time, set the MTD threshold, derive RTO & RPO targets)\n18* BIA definition: process to identify and evaluate effects of a disruption on critical business operations; key foundation of BCP/DRP planning\n19* BIA core question: \"What happens to the business — financially, operationally, legally, and reputationally — if this process stops working?\"\n20* BIA vs Risk Assessment distinction (RA asks \"What could go wrong?\"; BIA asks \"What's the impact, and how quickly must we recover?\")\n21* Why BIA matters (identifies mission-critical vs deferrable functions, quantifies impact, justifies budget, prevents over/under-investing, may be required for compliance, builds resilience, aligns business/IT/leadership)\n22* BIA key objectives (identify critical processes and dependencies, determine how impact escalates over time, establish recovery priorities, identify resource requirements, surface hidden interdependencies and SPOFs, provide shared understanding of \"critical\")\n23* MBCO: Minimum Business Continuity Objective — the minimum service level the business must maintain during a disruption\n24* The BIA process 7 steps (define scope, identify critical functions, map dependencies, assess impact at 7 increasing time intervals, assign RTO/RPO/MTD, prioritize functions for recovery, document findings and validate) - sequence question\n25* The 6 types of impact assessed (financial, operational, legal/regulatory, reputational, contractual, strategic) - scenario mapping question\n26* BIA common tools & techniques (structured interviews & workshops, BIA questionnaires/surveys, dependency mapping, impact-over-time analysis, criticality ranking matrices, BCM software)\n27* Plan review: why (a continuity plan is only accurate the day it is written; people/systems/risk change) - \"plans decay\" concept\n28* When to trigger a review (scheduled interval typically annual, organizational or technology change, after an incident or audit finding)\n29* The review process 5 steps (confirm scope & ownership, validate contacts & dependencies, cross-check against the BIA, update procedures, sign-off & version control)\n30* Roles & responsibilities (senior management owns and approves; BC manager coordinates; department heads validate local procedures; internal audit provides independent assurance)\n31* Why test the plan (validates assumptions, uncovers gaps, trains staff, satisfies compliance)\n32* The testing cycle (plan/document -> test/exercise -> review/capture results -> update/fold lessons) and testing methods range (tabletop to full interruption testing)\n33* Key takeaways (shared terminology; MTD is the ceiling; BIA turns disruption into numbers and numbers into priorities; plans decay so review; testing proves the plan)\n\n=== QUESTION QUALITY RULES (HARD) ===\n- Exactly 4 choices per question, exactly ONE correct answer (index 0=A,1=B,2=C,3=D). No \"all of the above\", no \"none of the above\", no two-correct answers, nothing a knowledgeable student could dispute.\n- Choice parity: all four choices must be similar in length (word/character count), same level of specificity, technicality, grammar, and professional tone. The correct answer must NEVER be the longest, most detailed, most specific, or most technical option. Rotate which position carries the longer text so length is not a clue.\n- No keyword leakage: do not repeat a distinctive phrase from the question inside the correct choice unless the distractors contain it too.\n- Distractors must be plausible: use nearby metrics (swap RTO/RPO/MTD/WRT roles), nearby definitions, or common misconceptions. Never absurd distractors.\n- Avoid absolute words (always/never/only/completely/guaranteed) unless semantically justified and balanced across options.\n- Question wording: plain and direct. Vary cognitive level: ~35% recall/understanding, 35% application, 25% analysis, 5% evaluation. Use scenarios (e.g., \"a trading system tolerates a 4-hour outage while payroll tolerates days — what metric is being set?\"), comparisons, exceptions (\"Which is NOT...\"), and sequences. Mark negative wording clearly.\n- Every question needs a 1-3 sentence explanation stating why the correct answer is right and briefly why the others are not, grounded in the reference.\n- ANSWER DISTRIBUTION (HARD): your 30 correctAnswers must be exactly A=8, B=7, C=8, D=7 (0=A,1=B,2=C,3=D). No letter appears more than 2 consecutive questions. No cycling pattern like A,B,C,D,A,B,C,D.\n- Check JSON validity before finishing.\n\n=== ESCAPING NOTE ===\nStrings are plain UTF-8 text; prefer ASCII-friendly phrasing. Escape internal double quotes with \\\". No backslashes otherwise.\n\n=== RETURN ===\nDo NOT print the JSON. In your final reply return only: (1) number of questions written, (2) exact correctAnswer letter distribution, (3) one-line confirmation that both files exist and are valid JSON, (4) any concept you had to drop and why.","subagent_type":"general"},"output":"<task id=\"ses_f428b7e50ffeX6rEi2P7DP4Cu9\" state=\"completed\">\n<task_result>\nAll validations pass. Final report:\n\n1. **Questions written:** 30\n2. **correctAnswer distribution:** A=8, B=7, C=8, D=7 (A8/B7/C8/D7 — no 3-in-a-row, no A,B,C,D rotation cycles)\n3. **Files:** `ch5.json` (1 chapter, 30 questions, 4 choices each) and `enc5.json` (12 entries) both written and confirmed valid JSON.\n4. **Dropped/merged concepts:** #12 (MTD=MTPD — folded into the Q12 explanation rather than its own question, being a naming fact), #21 (Why BIA matters — not asked standalone; its substance overlaps with the BIA objectives question Q20 and they can't both fit the 30-question cap), #33 (Key takeaways — pure recap too thin for a fair standalone question). All other starred concepts each map to exactly one question.\n</task_result>\n</task>","metadata":{"parentSessionId":"ses_f428ff92affeQ6wiY0s4pQPZG0","sessionId":"ses_f428b7e50ffeX6rEi2P7DP4Cu9","model":{"modelID":"big-pickle","providerID":"opencode"},"truncated":false},"title":"Generate ch5 question bank","time":{"start":1789884924339,"end":1789885457222}},"id":"prt_0bd744fa3001Fc1ymEfUnzjKi7","sessionID":"ses_f428ff92affeQ6wiY0s4pQPZG0","messageID":"msg_0bd7365d60013qmLhoE7nzwi0N"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1789885481771,"sessionID":"ses_f428ff92affeQ6wiY0s4pQPZG0","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"task","callID":"call_a9761682d99040fd87f29ab8","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"description":"Generate ch3 question bank","prompt":"You are building CHAPTER 3 of a 6-chapter interactive exam system. You are doing RESEARCH AND AUTHORING only — your ONLY deliverable is two JSON files written to disk with the Write tool.\n\nSOURCE OF TRUTH (read it with your Read tool before writing anything):\nC:\\xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\data\\exams\\rkq7bk65si88kk\\source.txt\nIt is a plain-text extraction of an \"IAS 2 Information Assurance & Security 2\" reviewer PDF. Chapter 3 corresponds to LESSON L5 \"Post Audit Activities (Deep Dive)\", which lives on the pages marked \"PAGE 9\" and \"PAGE 10\" of that file (approx lines 349-435). Focus on those pages and ONLY on the DEER-DIVE specifics they add on top of the basic lesson. Everything you write MUST be grounded in that text. NEVER invent facts.\n\nNOTE: another chapter covers the BASIC exit interview/data analysis/audit report material. DO NOT duplicate basic recall — your questions must test the DEEP-DIVE specifics: format details, the 5 key parts, presentation strategies, qualities, finding components, the 6 report qualities, deliverables, CAPs, and the three real-world audit examples.\n\nOUTPUT FILES (absolute paths; the parent folder already exists):\n1) C:\\Users\\kelly\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\opencode\\ias2build\\ch3.json\n   Content: a JSON ARRAY containing exactly ONE chapter object:\n   [\n     {\n       \"chapterId\": \"ch3\",\n       \"title\": \"L5: Post Audit Activities (Deep Dive)\",\n       \"description\": \"This chapter will deepen the approach to exit interviews, data analysis, audit reports, and presentation of findings, applying them through real-world audit examples.\",\n       \"questions\": [\n         {\n           \"id\": \"ch3-q01\",\n           \"question\": \"...\",\n           \"choices\": [\"...\", \"...\", \"...\", \"...\"],\n           \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n           \"explanation\": \"...\"\n         }\n       ]\n     }\n   ]\n   The array must be valid JSON. Use double quotes, escape internal quotes, no trailing commas.\n\n2) C:\\Users\\kelly\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\opencode\\ias2build\\enc3.json\n   Content: a JSON ARRAY of 10-12 encyclopedia entries for this chapter:\n   [\n     { \"chapterId\": \"ch3\", \"term\": \"Corrective Action Plan\", \"definition\": \"Definition grounded in the reference.\" }\n   ]\n\n=== REQUIRED COVERAGE ===\nProduce EXACTLY 30 questions, each drawing from a different concept below (each concept exactly once; do NOT ask the same fact twice with different wording). Cover at minimum the concepts marked with *:\n1* Exit interview (deep dive) definition: the final formal meeting before the official audit report is published\n2* Core concept: an initial, verbal debriefing session (execution of audit, initial observations, preliminary findings)\n3* Context: transition point from active field testing/evidence collection to formal reporting and remediation planning\n4* Purpose: prevent \"no surprises\", fact verification & validation, provide operational context, align expectations\n5* Format: structured high-level briefing, in-person or secure recorded video, 30-60 minutes, supported by an executive summary slide deck or position paper\n6* The 5 key parts of the meeting: executive opening & scope review; positive observations; preliminary risk & finding highlights (Critical/High/Medium); interactive discussion/management feedback; next steps & deliverable timeline\n7* Presentation strategy: focus on business impact (translate technical exploits into tangible business risk)\n8* Presentation strategy: collaborative tone (frames findings as opportunities to strengthen defense-in-depth, not blame)\n9* Presentation strategy: balanced delivery (open with effective controls before vulnerabilities to reduce defensiveness)\n10* The 4 qualities of a good exit interview (objective & evidence-based, transparent, constructive, interactive) - scenario match (e.g., which one means \"clear about what was tested and limitations\")\n11* Data analysis (deep dive) definition: systematic process of inspecting, cleaning, transforming, and evaluating raw data and security evidence\n12* Data analysis purpose: identify root causes, risk prioritization (likelihood vs impact), verify compliance (ISO 27001, NIST, PCI-DSS), eliminate false positives\n13* Data analysis tools: SIEM software, log parsers, script-based data filters, risk matrix models\n14* The 5 key parts of data analysis: data normalization & cleaning; correlation & pattern analysis; severity assessment & scoring (e.g., CVSS); root cause breakdown; trend & historical comparison\n15* Data analysis presentation strategy: leverage visual data representations, structure around narrative attack paths, distinguish confirmed vs potential risks, focus on actionable metrics\n16* The 4 qualities of good data analysis (accurate & defensible, context-aware, reproducible, outcome-oriented)\n17* Audit report (deep dive) definition: compiles evidence, analysis, observations, findings into a formal written document; communicates the overall conclusion or opinion\n18* Finding components: the 5 core elements (Condition, Criteria, Cause, Effect/Risk, Recommendation) - include a scenario that asks you to identify which element a given statement represents\n19* The 6 qualities of a good audit report (accurate, objective, clear, constructive, timely, confidential)\n20* Audit workflow progression (audit evidence -> analysis -> findings -> opinion/conclusion -> audit report) - sequence\n21* Presentation of findings (deep dive): strategic role (connects technical analysis with executive decision-making and remediation)\n22* Deliverables: summarizes control gaps, operational risks, policy non-compliances; prioritizes via severity ratings (High, Medium, Low); outlines Corrective Action Plans with deadlines and accountability\n23* Advantages of presenting findings (immediate clarity & direct dialogue, faster stakeholder buy-in, shared accountability, strategic value addition)\n24* Disadvantages (defensiveness & conflict, misinterpretation risk, time & resource intensive, scope creep during discussion)\n25* Example 1 (IT & cybersecurity audit): finding = 58% of workstations missing critical security patches + default admin credentials on network switches; impact = ransomware risk; fix = mandatory 14-day automated patch deployment plan\n26* Example 2 (financial compliance & inventory): finding = 12% discrepancy between ERP physical inventory logs and warehouse cycle counts due to delayed manual data entry; fix = barcode-scanning workflow\n27* Example 3 (quality & safety ISO/HACCP): finding = Cold Room B exceeded 4°C three times in the quarter without documented corrective actions; fix = automated IoT temperature-alerting framework\n28 The distinction between the basic L3 exit interview definition and the L5 deep-dive framing (comparison question)\n29 \"Objective & evidence-based\" quality: grounded in verifiable logs, screenshots, or configuration files—never assumptions or personal opinion\n30 \"Interactive\" quality: encourages open dialogue, allows system owners to provide context without fear of immediate dismissal\n\n=== QUESTION QUALITY RULES (HARD) ===\n- Exactly 4 choices per question, exactly ONE correct answer (index 0=A,1=B,2=C,3=D). No \"all of the above\", no \"none of the above\", no two-correct answers, nothing a knowledgeable student could dispute.\n- Choice parity: all four choices must be similar in length (word/character count), same level of specificity, technicality, grammar, and professional tone. The correct answer must NEVER be the longest, most detailed, most specific, or most technical option. Rotate which position carries the longer text so length is not a clue.\n- No keyword leakage: do not repeat a distinctive phrase from the question inside the correct choice unless the distractors contain it too.\n- Distractors must be plausible: use nearby concepts from this lesson (other qualities, other steps, other examples) or common misconceptions. Never absurd distractors.\n- Avoid absolute words (always/never/only/completely/guaranteed) unless semantically justified and balanced across options.\n- Question wording: plain and direct. Vary cognitive level: ~35% recall/understanding, 35% application, 25% analysis, 5% evaluation. Use scenarios (particularly the three real-world audit examples and the 5 key parts), comparisons, exceptions (\"Which is NOT...\"), and sequences. Mark negative wording clearly.\n- Every question needs a 1-3 sentence explanation stating why the correct answer is right and briefly why the others are not, grounded in the reference.\n- ANSWER DISTRIBUTION (HARD): your 30 correctAnswers must be exactly A=8, B=7, C=7, D=8 (0=A,1=B,2=C,3=D). No letter appears more than 2 consecutive questions. No cycling pattern like A,B,C,D,A,B,C,D.\n- Check JSON validity before finishing.\n\n=== ESCAPING NOTE ===\nStrings are plain UTF-8 text; prefer ASCII-friendly phrasing. Escape internal double quotes with \\\". No backslashes otherwise.\n\n=== RETURN ===\nDo NOT print the JSON. In your final reply return only: (1) number of questions written, (2) exact correctAnswer letter distribution, (3) one-line confirmation that both files exist and are valid JSON, (4) any concept you had to drop and why.","subagent_type":"general"},"output":"<task id=\"ses_f428be014fferuf9gHFwGxsK4T\" state=\"completed\">\n<task_result>\nBoth files written and validated.\n\n1. Questions written: **30**\n2. correctAnswer distribution: **A=8, B=7, C=7, D=8** (sequence `ACBDACDBCABDACBADCBDBDBCADACAD`; no 3-in-a-row, no cycling)\n3. Both `ch3.json` (30 questions) and `enc3.json` (12 encyclopedia entries) exist and parse as valid JSON.\n4. No concepts dropped — all 30 required concepts covered. One encyclopedia term (\"Exit Interview Purpose\") was merged into the \"Exit Interview (Deep Dive)\" entry to stay within the 10–12 entry limit.\n</task_result>\n</task>","metadata":{"parentSessionId":"ses_f428ff92affeQ6wiY0s4pQPZG0","sessionId":"ses_f428be014fferuf9gHFwGxsK4T","model":{"modelID":"big-pickle","providerID":"opencode"},"truncated":false},"title":"Generate ch3 question bank","time":{"start":1789884899310,"end":1789885481767}},"id":"prt_0bd73e024001wYnQp7IK7gVhPp","sessionID":"ses_f428ff92affeQ6wiY0s4pQPZG0","messageID":"msg_0bd7365d60013qmLhoE7nzwi0N"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1789885623695,"sessionID":"ses_f428ff92affeQ6wiY0s4pQPZG0","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"task","callID":"call_bedc7bc13f6c46949cd32aef","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"description":"Generate ch2 question bank","prompt":"You are building CHAPTER 2 of a 6-chapter interactive exam system. You are doing RESEARCH AND AUTHORING only — your ONLY deliverable is two JSON files written to disk with the Write tool.\n\nSOURCE OF TRUTH (read it with your Read tool before writing anything):\nC:\\xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\data\\exams\\rkq7bk65si88kk\\source.txt\nIt is a plain-text extraction of an \"IAS 2 Information Assurance & Security 2\" reviewer PDF. Chapter 2 corresponds to LESSON L4 \"Log Information and Security Controls\", which lives on the pages marked \"PAGE 5\", \"PAGE 6\", \"PAGE 7\", and \"PAGE 8\" of that file (approx lines 156-348). Focus on those pages. Everything you write MUST be grounded in that text. NEVER invent facts. If a concept is too thin to support a question, do not force it.\n\nOUTPUT FILES (absolute paths; the parent folder already exists):\n1) C:\\Users\\kelly\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\opencode\\ias2build\\ch2.json\n   Content: a JSON ARRAY containing exactly ONE chapter object:\n   [\n     {\n       \"chapterId\": \"ch2\",\n       \"title\": \"L4: Log Information and Security Controls\",\n       \"description\": \"This chapter will have a deeper focus on logging and security controls such as IDS, NAC, host isolation, hardening, and testing - the student should distinguish the distinct detection methods and log information types.\",\n       \"questions\": [\n         {\n           \"id\": \"ch2-q01\",\n           \"question\": \"...\",\n           \"choices\": [\"...\", \"...\", \"...\", \"...\"],\n           \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n           \"explanation\": \"...\"\n         }\n       ]\n     }\n   ]\n   The array must be valid JSON. Use double quotes, escape internal quotes, no trailing commas.\n\n2) C:\\Users\\kelly\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\opencode\\ias2build\\enc2.json\n   Content: a JSON ARRAY of 10-12 encyclopedia entries for this chapter:\n   [\n     { \"chapterId\": \"ch2\", \"term\": \"IDS\", \"definition\": \"Definition grounded in the reference.\" }\n   ]\n\n=== REQUIRED COVERAGE ===\nProduce EXACTLY 30 questions, each drawing from a different concept below (each concept exactly once; do NOT ask the same fact twice with different wording). Cover at minimum the concepts marked with *:\n1* Security logging definition and the 4 reasons organizations use logs (detect unauthorized access, investigate incidents, verify controls, support audits/compliance)\n2* The 4 log questions: WHO / WHAT / WHEN / WHERE\n3* Authentication logs content (successful/failed logins, password changes, MFA events, account lockouts)\n4* Authorization logs content (restricted file access, privilege changes, administrative actions)\n5* Network logs content (firewall connections, blocked traffic, VPN activity, network scans)\n6* System and application logs content; NIST IDPS log fields (timestamp, event type, event source, sensor or reporting system)\n7* IDS definition (monitors, generates alerts rather than blocking)\n8* NIDS vs HIDS (what each monitors)\n9* IDS log information captured (alert ID, date/time, source/dest IPs, ports, protocol, detection rule, suspected attack type, severity, affected host/account/app, sensor, response taken)\n10* The 4 IDS detection methods (signature-based, anomaly-based, protocol analysis, event correlation)\n11* Verifying security controls (authorized failed-login/test intrusion; confirm log entry, correct IDS alert, alerts reach SIEM; validate timestamps; review false positives; document results)\n12* Protecting logs: centralized SIEM, restrict access, encryption in transit and storage, separate log storage from generating systems\n13* Log integrity protections (hashes, digital signatures, append-only storage); monitor modification/deletion attempts and log sources that stop sending\n14* Log review: continuous or documented schedule; red flags to investigate (repeated failed logins, privilege escalation, disabled security services, malware detection, unexpected admin activity, large/unusual data transfers)\n15* The 6 steps when suspicious activity is identified (validate alert, identify affected systems, preserve evidence, contain, investigate & document, improve controls) - sequence question\n16* Signature-based analysis: def + pros (accurate for known threats, few false positives) + con (blind to zero-day/morphing malware)\n17* Anomaly-based analysis: def (ML/statistical baseline) + pro (detects novel/zero-day) + con (high false positives)\n18* Stateful protocol analysis: def (vendor profiles + state tracking) + pro (catches protocol manipulation/illegal commands/scans) + con (heavy resource overhead); how it differs from anomaly detection\n19* Threshold monitoring, resource profiling, executable profiling definitions\n20* HIDS definition and its 4 working parts (data collection, FIM, analysis & matching, alerting & logging)\n21* File Integrity Monitoring (FIM) purpose in HIDS (baseline snapshots vs current state)\n22* Layered defense (defense in depth) definition and the 7 layers; include at least one mapping question (e.g., which layer antivirus/EDR/HIDS belongs to, which controls protect physical access)\n23* NAC definition and what it evaluates (user identity, device identity, security posture, network requirements)\n24* The 4 NAC decisions: allow, deny, restrict, quarantine\n25* The NAC 5-step process (identification -> authentication -> security assessment -> authorization -> monitoring) - sequence question\n26* NAC verification scenarios (authorized user, unauthorized user, unknown device, non-compliant device, disabled account)\n27* Host isolation definition + primary purpose (containment) + why not immediate shutdown (investigators can still examine)\n28* Indicators prompting host isolation (malware detection, suspicious processes, abnormal connections, credential compromise, data exfiltration, unauthorized system changes, IDS/HIDS alerts)\n29* The 5 objectives of host isolation (containment, protection, investigation, evidence preservation, recovery) and the 6 verifying steps\n30* System hardening definition, why it matters (unnecessary services, outdated software, weak passwords, excessive privileges), 5 hardening goals\n31* Security monitoring vs testing difference; why regularly tested (new vulnerabilities appear); the 4 testing methods (vulnerability scanning, penetration testing, security audits, log and alert testing)\n\n=== QUESTION QUALITY RULES (HARD) ===\n- Exactly 4 choices per question, exactly ONE correct answer (index 0=A,1=B,2=C,3=D). No \"all of the above\", no \"none of the above\", no two-correct answers, nothing a knowledgeable student could dispute.\n- Choice parity: all four choices must be similar in length (word/character count), same level of specificity, technicality, grammar, and professional tone. The correct answer must NEVER be the longest, most detailed, most specific, or most technical option. Rotate which position carries the longer text so length is not a clue.\n- No keyword leakage: do not repeat a distinctive phrase from the question inside the correct choice unless the distractors contain it too.\n- Distractors must be plausible: use nearby concepts (e.g., mix up NIDS/HIDS, detection methods, log types) or common misconceptions. Never absurd distractors.\n- Avoid absolute words (always/never/only/completely/guaranteed) unless semantically justified and balanced across options.\n- Question wording: plain and direct. Vary cognitive level: ~40% recall/understanding, 30% application, 20% analysis, 10% evaluation. Use scenarios (e.g., an analyst sees repeated failed logins; a NAC decision scenario), comparisons (NIDS vs HIDS, signature vs anomaly), exceptions (\"Which is NOT...\"), and sequences. Mark negative wording clearly.\n- Every question needs a 1-3 sentence explanation stating why the correct answer is right and briefly why the others are not, grounded in the reference.\n- ANSWER DISTRIBUTION (HARD): your 30 correctAnswers must be exactly A=7, B=8, C=8, D=7 (0=A,1=B,2=C,3=D). No letter appears more than 2 consecutive questions. No cycling pattern like A,B,C,D,A,B,C,D.\n- Check JSON validity before finishing.\n\n=== ESCAPING NOTE ===\nStrings are plain UTF-8 text; prefer ASCII-friendly phrasing. Escape internal double quotes with \\\". No backslashes otherwise.\n\n=== RETURN ===\nDo NOT print the JSON. In your final reply return only: (1) number of questions written, (2) exact correctAnswer letter distribution, (3) one-line confirmation that both files exist and are valid JSON, (4) any concept you had to drop and why.","subagent_type":"general"},"output":"<task id=\"ses_f428c2154ffeobMIVztrJMya4j\" state=\"completed\">\n<task_result>\nBoth files are written and validated:\n\n1. **Questions written:** 30\n2. **Answer distribution:** A(0)=7, B(1)=8, C(2)=8, D(3)=7 — exactly as required; max consecutive run = 2; no cycling pattern\n3. **Both files exist and parse as valid JSON:** `ch2.json` (30 questions, sequential IDs ch2-q01..ch2-q30, exactly 4 choices each) and `enc2.json` (12 encyclopedia entries)\n4. **Dropped concept:** concept 10 (the standalone \"4 IDS detection methods\" list). Dropped because concepts 16/17/18 each already cover signature-based, anomaly-based, and stateful protocol analysis in depth, and the unique item (event correlation) is preserved in the encyclopedia entry \"IDS Log Information and Detection Methods\", so no fact is truly lost.\n</task_result>\n</task>","metadata":{"parentSessionId":"ses_f428ff92affeQ6wiY0s4pQPZG0","sessionId":"ses_f428c2154ffeobMIVztrJMya4j","model":{"modelID":"big-pickle","providerID":"opencode"},"truncated":false},"title":"Generate ch2 question bank","time":{"start":1789884882606,"end":1789885623692}},"id":"prt_0bd73a230001fXygBcJefvQIoh","sessionID":"ses_f428ff92affeQ6wiY0s4pQPZG0","messageID":"msg_0bd7365d60013qmLhoE7nzwi0N"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1789885644050,"sessionID":"ses_f428ff92affeQ6wiY0s4pQPZG0","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"task","callID":"call_42aae047b66741719f36dc28","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"description":"Generate ch6 question bank","prompt":"You are building CHAPTER 6 of a 6-chapter interactive exam system. You are doing RESEARCH AND AUTHORING only — your ONLY deliverable is two JSON files written to disk with the Write tool.\n\nSOURCE OF TRUTH (read it with your Read tool before writing anything):\nC:\\xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\data\\exams\\rkq7bk65si88kk\\source.txt\nIt is a plain-text extraction of an \"IAS 2 Information Assurance & Security 2\" reviewer PDF. Chapter 6 corresponds to LESSON L8 \"Backing Up Data and Application and Incident Handling\", which lives on the pages marked \"PAGE 17\", \"PAGE 18\", \"PAGE 19\", and \"PAGE 20\" of that file (approx lines 698-895). Focus on those pages. Everything you write MUST be grounded in that text. NEVER invent facts. If a concept is too thin to support a question, do not force it.\n\nOUTPUT FILES (absolute paths; the parent folder already exists):\n1) C:\\Users\\kelly\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\opencode\\ias2build\\ch6.json\n   Content: a JSON ARRAY containing exactly ONE chapter object:\n   [\n     {\n       \"chapterId\": \"ch6\",\n       \"title\": \"L8: Backing Up Data and Applications & Incident Handling\",\n       \"description\": \"This chapter covers backup types (full, incremental, differential, mirror) and the incident handling lifecycle: preparation, identification, notification, response, recovery, post-incident review, documentation, and reporting.\",\n       \"questions\": [\n         {\n           \"id\": \"ch6-q01\",\n           \"question\": \"...\",\n           \"choices\": [\"...\", \"...\", \"...\", \"...\"],\n           \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n           \"explanation\": \"...\"\n         }\n       ]\n     }\n   ]\n   The array must be valid JSON. Use double quotes, escape internal quotes, no trailing commas.\n\n2) C:\\Users\\kelly\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\opencode\\ias2build\\enc6.json\n   Content: a JSON ARRAY of 10-12 encyclopedia entries for this chapter:\n   [\n     { \"chapterId\": \"ch6\", \"term\": \"Jump Kit\", \"definition\": \"Definition grounded in the reference.\" }\n   ]\n\n=== REQUIRED COVERAGE ===\nProduce EXACTLY 30 questions, each drawing from a different concept below (each concept exactly once; do NOT ask the same fact twice with different wording). Cover at minimum the concepts marked with *:\n1* Backup definition + the three tradeoffs (speed, storage space, recovery time)\n2* Full backup: copies all data; slowest to create, fastest to restore, most storage\n3* Incremental backup: copies data changed since the last backup of ANY kind; fastest to create, slowest to restore (multiple sets needed), least storage\n4* Differential backup: copies data changed since the last FULL backup; grows larger over time than incremental; faster restore (full + latest differential only)\n5* Mirror backup: exact real-time copy, continuously updated; no version history; no rollback protection (deleted from source = removed from mirror)\n6* Incident Handling Phase 1 Preparation: goal (get the organization ready before an incident happens; reduces damage and response time)\n7* Preparation - policies & plans (incident response policy, incident response plan/IRP, roles & responsibilities of the IR team, communication & escalation procedures)\n8* Preparation - tools & resources (deploy security tools like IDS/IPS, firewalls, antivirus, logging; updated hardware/software inventory; forensic tools and \"jump kit\"; backup systems in place and tested)\n9* Preparation - training & testing (ongoing cycle: employee awareness training, tabletop drills/simulations, testing communication channels, periodic plan review)\n10* Phase 2 Identification: overview (separates true security incidents from false alarms; determines scope, nature, severity; early accurate identification limits damage)\n11* Detection sources (alerts from IDS/IPS, antivirus, SIEM; log analysis; employee reports; system/network performance anomalies)\n12* Verification steps (confirm it is a real incident; gather initial evidence: timestamps, affected systems, indicators; classify type & severity; document findings as discovered - chain of custody)\n13* Next actions after confirmation (notify the IR team, assign handler/owner, preserve evidence, move to containment once confirmed)\n14* The three-phase response model: Notification -> Response -> Recovery (sequence)\n15* Notification: who should be informed - the 5 groups (internal IT & security teams, management & executives, legal & compliance officers, affected users & customers, law enforcement & regulators)\n16* Reporting the incident - the 4 principles (standardized reports, defined communication, audit trail logging, factual accuracy - avoid speculation)\n17* Why timely notification matters (limits damage, meets legal deadlines e.g., 72 hours, preserves trust, speeds up recovery)\n18* Response - containing the incident (isolate affected systems to stop lateral movement, disable compromised accounts, apply short-term fixes like blocking IPs/closing ports/emergency firewall rules, preserve evidence before changing anything)\n19* Preventing further damage - the 4 steps (continuous monitoring, patch vulnerabilities, segment networks, staff communication)\n20* Eradication - removing the cause, 4 steps (root cause identification, malware eradication incl. backdoors & unauthorized accounts, close vulnerabilities, eradication verification with scans and audits)\n21* Recovery - restoring systems and data from backups (never data that may still be compromised; identify a clean restore point taken before the incident and confirmed malware-free; rebuild on trusted media; restore in priority order - critical systems first)\n22* Testing if restored systems work properly - the 4 steps (verify functionality, security scans for residual threat, performance check under normal load, obtain sign-off from IT/security/business owners)\n23* Returning to normal operations - 4 steps (gradual rollout with close monitoring, notify stakeholders, resume monitoring routines, lessons learned and IR plan update)\n24* Post Incident Review: the 4 key questions (what happened and when; were documented procedures followed and were they adequate; what slowed recovery; what do we do differently next time)\n25* Find the real cause, not the symptom (the server-down symptom vs nobody watching disk space/never tested restore; the \"5 Whys\" method)\n26* Checking if the backup really worked (how much data lost vs affordable = RPO; how long down vs fast enough = RTO; a damaged or three-weeks-old backup is itself a finding; every finding needs a person in charge and a due date)\n27* Numbers to track: MTTD = mean time to detect (how long to notice the problem); MTTR = mean time to remediate/resolve (how long to fix)\n28* Documentation: starts the moment you detect the incident - a running log written DURING, not after, the event\n29* The running log's 8 key items (incident ID, discovery, scope, severity, actions, notifications, evidence, backup used)\n30* Documentation golden rule (write facts only, never guesses) + legal status (the log is evidence usable in court, insurance claims, government audits; keep access restricted, keep per policy)\n31* Reporting: one incident, five readers (technical team: full timeline/real cause/fixes; management: short 1-2 page summary with cost/downtime/business impact; legal team & regulator: official breach notice before the legal deadline; affected users: plain language; law enforcement: only when a crime was committed)\n32* The written report's 10 parts, with two specific facts: the Executive Summary is written last but read first; the Impact Assessment section is the one managers read twice (justifies budget: money lost, downtime, data lost, reputation damage)\n33* Republic Act 10173 (Data Privacy Act of 2012) & NPC Circular 16-03: 72 hours to notify the NPC and affected people (clock starts when you know or have good reason to believe a breach happened, not when investigation finishes); 5 days to submit the full breach report; notice must state what happened, what data was involved, what was done to reduce harm, and who to contact (e.g., Data Protection Officer); H&M Philippines was ordered by the NPC to explain missing the 72-hour deadline after a 2018 payment card incident\n\n=== QUESTION QUALITY RULES (HARD) ===\n- Exactly 4 choices per question, exactly ONE correct answer (index 0=A,1=B,2=C,3=D). No \"all of the above\", no \"none of the above\", no two-correct answers, nothing a knowledgeable student could dispute.\n- Choice parity: all four choices must be similar in length (word/character count), same level of specificity, technicality, grammar, and professional tone. The correct answer must NEVER be the longest, most detailed, most specific, or most technical option. Rotate which position carries the longer text so length is not a clue.\n- No keyword leakage: do not repeat a distinctive phrase from the question inside the correct choice unless the distractors contain it too.\n- Distractors must be plausible: use nearby concepts (other backup types, other phases, other steps, other notification groups) or common misconceptions. Never absurd distractors.\n- Avoid absolute words (always/never/only/completely/guaranteed) unless semantically justified and balanced across options.\n- Question wording: plain and direct. Vary cognitive level: ~35% recall/understanding, 35% application, 25% analysis, 5% evaluation. Use scenarios (recovery decisions, backup choice given constraints, a breach-notification timeline problem), comparisons (backup types; MTTD vs MTTR), exceptions (\"Which is NOT...\"), and sequences. Mark negative wording clearly.\n- Every question needs a 1-3 sentence explanation stating why the correct answer is right and briefly why the others are not, grounded in the reference.\n- ANSWER DISTRIBUTION (HARD): your 30 correctAnswers must be exactly A=7, B=8, C=7, D=8 (0=A,1=B,2=C,3=D). No letter appears more than 2 consecutive questions. No cycling pattern like A,B,C,D,A,B,C,D.\n- Check JSON validity before finishing.\n\n=== ESCAPING NOTE ===\nStrings are plain UTF-8 text; prefer ASCII-friendly phrasing. Escape internal double quotes with \\\". No backslashes otherwise.\n\n=== RETURN ===\nDo NOT print the JSON. In your final reply return only: (1) number of questions written, (2) exact correctAnswer letter distribution, (3) one-line confirmation that both files exist and are valid JSON, (4) any concept you had to drop and why.","subagent_type":"general"},"output":"<task id=\"ses_f428b450affe4owEkBOuIcBBrV\" state=\"completed\">\n<task_result>\nDone.\n\n1. **Questions written:** 30\n2. **correctAnswer distribution:** A=7, B=8, C=7, D=8 (sequence `BADCBADBCABDACDBACDBCADBCADCBD`, max consecutive = 1, no ABCD cycle)\n3. **Files:** `ch6.json` and `enc6.json` both exist, parse as valid JSON (30 questions in one chapter object; 12 encyclopedia entries).\n4. **Concepts dropped (merged into adjacent explanations, not forced as standalone questions):**\n   - Concept 13 (next actions after confirmation) — folded into q12's explanation; it is a short transition step immediately following verification.\n   - Concept 23 (returning to normal operations, 4 steps) — folded into q21's explanation; overlaps heavily with recovery/testing and lessons-learned material.\n   - Concept 30 (documentation golden rule + legal status) — folded into q26's explanation; two-fact concept too thin to stand alone without redundancy with the running-log question.\n</task_result>\n</task>","metadata":{"parentSessionId":"ses_f428ff92affeQ6wiY0s4pQPZG0","sessionId":"ses_f428b450affe4owEkBOuIcBBrV","model":{"modelID":"big-pickle","providerID":"opencode"},"truncated":false},"title":"Generate ch6 question bank","time":{"start":1789884939003,"end":1789885644029}},"id":"prt_0bd7481c7001Q4yV57w6hyVESH","sessionID":"ses_f428ff92affeQ6wiY0s4pQPZG0","messageID":"msg_0bd7365d60013qmLhoE7nzwi0N"}}
