{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1789885938967,"sessionID":"ses_f428ff92affeQ6wiY0s4pQPZG0","part":{"id":"prt_0bd83fd160016QWxJlldCj3GI5","messageID":"msg_0bd83f3640017Tko3NVYhS8Gpx","sessionID":"ses_f428ff92affeQ6wiY0s4pQPZG0","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1789885939560,"sessionID":"ses_f428ff92affeQ6wiY0s4pQPZG0","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_003ebaf8aa014831b3c10490","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"& \"C:\\xampp\\php\\php.exe\" \"C:\\Users\\kelly\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\opencode\\ias2build\\spot.php\""},"output":"=== ch1-q03 (correct=B) ===\nQ: An exit interview, also called an exit or closing meeting, is held at which point in the audit process?\n  [A] Right after the final audit report is released to the board of directors.\n  [B] At the conclusion of fieldwork, when necessary, before the report is released.\n  [C] At the start of fieldwork to confirm the scope and timetable with management first.\n  [D] Whenever the financial statements show a suspected case of material fraud.\nEXPL: The lesson describes the exit interview as a meeting held at the conclusion of audit fieldwork, when necessary, before the final audit report is released. It is not held after publication, at the start of fieldwork, or only when fraud is suspected.\n\n=== ch1-q28 (correct=B) ===\nQ: An auditor is explaining the standard report format to a trainee and wants to stress the single most critical element. Which part should be emphasized?\n  [A] The title of the report.\n  [B] The opinion of the auditor.\n  [C] The addressee block.\n  [D] The date of the audit report.\nEXPL: The lesson marks the Opinion as the most critical content in an audit report because it states the impression derived after auditing. Title, addressee, and date are standard parts but are not flagged as most critical.\n\n=== ch2-q07 (correct=C) ===\nQ: Which of the following best describes how an intrusion detection system (IDS) behaves?\n  [A] Block all malicious traffic instantly\n  [B] Replace the company network firewall\n  [C] Monitor activity and generate alerts\n  [D] Encrypt traffic between all devices\nEXPL: An IDS monitors activity and detects possible attacks or policy violations, and it generally generates alerts rather than blocking activity automatically. Blocking traffic, replacing the firewall, and encrypting traffic describe different security controls.\n\n=== ch2-q22 (correct=B) ===\nQ: Which of the following can a Network Access Control (NAC) solution evaluate?\n  [A] Employee performance reviews\n  [B] User identity and credentials\n  [C] Office utility consumption\n  [D] Marketing campaign results\nEXPL: NAC can evaluate user identity (username, credentials, role), device identity (device type, MAC address, certificate, ownership), security posture (OS, updates, endpoint protection, configuration), and network requirements. Reviewing performance, utilities, or marketing is not a NAC function.\n\n=== ch3-q18 (correct=C) ===\nQ: An audit finding states that Server X was configured with default administrator credentials. Which finding component does this statement represent?\n  [A] Recommendation.\n  [B] Criteria.\n  [C] Condition.\n  [D] Cause.\nEXPL: Condition is what was found or observed, and the statement describes the observed state of the server. It is not the criteria, which is what should have been in place; the cause, which is why the issue occurred; or the recommendation, which is the action needed.\n\n=== ch4-q17 (correct=D) ===\nQ: A risk has a likelihood of 4 and an impact of 3 on a 1 to 5 scale. Using the fundamental formula, what is the risk level?\n  [A] 7, because the level is found by adding likelihood and impact.\n  [B] 1, because the level is found by subtracting impact from likelihood.\n  [C] 43, because the two values are joined into one number.\n  [D] 12, because the level equals likelihood multiplied by impact.\nEXPL: The fundamental formula is Risk Level = Likelihood x Impact, so 4 x 3 = 12. Adding, subtracting, or joining the digits are not how the risk level is computed.\n\n=== ch5-q05 (correct=B) ===\nQ: An organization must be able to restore its data to a point no more than one hour before a failure. Which metric is being set?\n  [A] The recovery time allowed to bring the application back online.\n  [B] The recovery point to which data must be restored.\n  [C] The maximum tolerable downtime for the business function.\n  [D] The work recovery period before normal processing resumes.\nEXPL: The RPO is the maximum acceptable amount of data loss, measured as a point in time to which data must be restored, so a one-hour target is an RPO. The first option is the RTO, the third is the MTD, and the fourth is the WRT.\n\n=== ch5-q24 (correct=C) ===\nQ: Which BIA technique visualizes how processes, systems, people, and vendors depend on one another?\n  [A] BIA questionnaires sent across the business units.\n  [B] Criticality ranking matrices used to compare processes.\n  [C] Dependency mapping of processes, systems, and vendors.\n  [D] Impact-over-time analysis of how disruption harm grows.\nEXPL: Dependency mapping visualizes how processes, systems, people, and vendors depend on each other. Questionnaires collect consistent data across units, matrices compare processes for prioritization, and impact-over-time analysis shows how harm grows.\n\n=== ch6-q09 (correct=C) ===\nQ: Which of the following is NOT part of the training and testing cycle in the Preparation phase?\n  [A] Running employee awareness training on security topics.\n  [B] Performing tabletop drills and simulations with the IR team.\n  [C] Maintaining continuous real-time mirror copies of the data.\n  [D] Testing communication channels and reviewing the plan periodically.\nEXPL: The ongoing cycle includes employee awareness training, tabletop drills and simulations, testing communication channels, and periodic plan review. Creating real-time mirror copies is a backup operation, not a training or testing activity.\n\n=== ch6-q27 (correct=D) ===\nQ: Which of the following is one of the eight key items written into the running log?\n  [A] Salaries, expenses, and the total project budget.\n  [B] The response team's attendance and break times.\n  [C] Marketing materials used during the incident.\n  [D] Discovery, scope, and the backup that was used.\nEXPL: The eight key items are the incident ID, discovery, scope, severity, actions, notifications, evidence, and the backup used. Salary, attendance, and marketing details are not among the required items.\n\n\n--- ENC SAMPLE (2 per chapter) ---\n[ch1] Post-Audit Activities: The activities performed after the audit has been conducted to review, analyze, document, and communicate the results of the audit. Their core purpose is to help the organization understand the audit findings, address the identified issues, and determine appropriate actions for improvement.\n[ch1] Exit Interview: A meeting held at the conclusion of audit fieldwork, also called an exit or closing meeting, used to discuss audit findings, resolve questions or concerns, and address outstanding issues before the final audit report is released. It is held when necessary rather than being mandatory for every audit.\n[ch2] Security Logging: Security logs record activities occurring on computers, networks, applications, and security devices. Organizations use logs for four main reasons: to detect unauthorized access, investigate security incidents, verify security controls, and support audits and compliance. Logs answer four important questions: WHO performed the action, WHAT happened, WHEN it happened, and WHERE it originated.\n[ch2] Log Types and NIST IDPS Fields: Authentication logs record successful and failed logins, password changes, multi-factor authentication events, and account lockouts. Authorization logs record access to restricted files, privilege changes, and administrative actions. Network logs record firewall connections, blocked traffic, VPN activity, and network scans. System and application logs record operating-system errors, software installations, configuration changes, application failures, and user transactions. NIST identifies the timestamp, event type, event source, and sensor or reporting system as important log fields for IDPS events.\n[ch3] Exit Interview (Deep Dive): The final formal meeting conducted between the security audit team and key organizational stakeholders or management before the official audit report is published. It is an initial verbal debriefing that discusses the execution of the audit, initial observations, and preliminary findings, and represents the transition from field testing and evidence collection to formal reporting and remediation planning.\n[ch3] Exit Interview Format: A structured, high-level briefing held in person or via secure, recorded video conference, typically lasting 30 to 60 minutes and supported by an executive summary slide deck or position paper.\n[ch4] Risk Identification: The process of finding, recognizing, and documenting potential risks that may affect a project; it is the starting point of the risk management process and is guided by asking what could go wrong while developing the system.\n[ch4] Brainstorming: A risk identification technique where team members identify risks such as lack of resources, technical errors, or delays.\n[ch5] Business Continuity Management (BCM): An organization-wide framework of policies, processes, and capabilities that prepare a business to prevent, respond to, and recover from disruptive incidents.\n[ch5] Business Continuity Plan (BCP): The documented set of procedures an organization follows to keep critical functions running during, and immediately after, a disruption.\n[ch6] Full Backup: A backup type that copies all data. It is the slowest to create and uses the most storage, but restores the fastest because only one backup set is needed.\n[ch6] Incremental Backup: A backup type that copies data changed since the last backup of any kind (full or incremental). It is the fastest to create and uses the least storage, but restoring takes longer because multiple backup sets are needed.\n","metadata":{"output":"=== ch1-q03 (correct=B) ===\nQ: An exit interview, also called an exit or closing meeting, is held at which point in the audit process?\n  [A] Right after the final audit report is released to the board of directors.\n  [B] At the conclusion of fieldwork, when necessary, before the report is released.\n  [C] At the start of fieldwork to confirm the scope and timetable with management first.\n  [D] Whenever the financial statements show a suspected case of material fraud.\nEXPL: The lesson describes the exit interview as a meeting held at the conclusion of audit fieldwork, when necessary, before the final audit report is released. It is not held after publication, at the start of fieldwork, or only when fraud is suspected.\n\n=== ch1-q28 (correct=B) ===\nQ: An auditor is explaining the standard report format to a trainee and wants to stress the single most critical element. Which part should be emphasized?\n  [A] The title of the report.\n  [B] The opinion of the auditor.\n  [C] The addressee block.\n  [D] The date of the audit report.\nEXPL: The lesson marks the Opinion as the most critical content in an audit report because it states the impression derived after auditing. Title, addressee, and date are standard parts but are not flagged as most critical.\n\n=== ch2-q07 (correct=C) ===\nQ: Which of the following best describes how an intrusion detection system (IDS) behaves?\n  [A] Block all malicious traffic instantly\n  [B] Replace the company network firewall\n  [C] Monitor activity and generate alerts\n  [D] Encrypt traffic between all devices\nEXPL: An IDS monitors activity and detects possible attacks or policy violations, and it generally generates alerts rather than blocking activity automatically. Blocking traffic, replacing the firewall, and encrypting traffic describe different security controls.\n\n=== ch2-q22 (correct=B) ===\nQ: Which of the following can a Network Access Control (NAC) solution evaluate?\n  [A] Employee performance reviews\n  [B] User identity and credentials\n  [C] Office utility consumption\n  [D] Marketing campaign results\nEXPL: NAC can evaluate user identity (username, credentials, role), device identity (device type, MAC address, certificate, ownership), security posture (OS, updates, endpoint protection, configuration), and network requirements. Reviewing performance, utilities, or marketing is not a NAC function.\n\n=== ch3-q18 (correct=C) ===\nQ: An audit finding states that Server X was configured with default administrator credentials. Which finding component does this statement represent?\n  [A] Recommendation.\n  [B] Criteria.\n  [C] Condition.\n  [D] Cause.\nEXPL: Condition is what was found or observed, and the statement describes the observed state of the server. It is not the criteria, which is what should have been in place; the cause, which is why the issue occurred; or the recommendation, which is the action needed.\n\n=== ch4-q17 (correct=D) ===\nQ: A risk has a likelihood of 4 and an impact of 3 on a 1 to 5 scale. Using the fundamental formula, what is the risk level?\n  [A] 7, because the level is found by adding likelihood and impact.\n  [B] 1, because the level is found by subtracting impact from likelihood.\n  [C] 43, because the two values are joined into one number.\n  [D] 12, because the level equals likelihood multiplied by impact.\nEXPL: The fundamental formula is Risk Level = Likelihood x Impact, so 4 x 3 = 12. Adding, subtracting, or joining the digits are not how the risk level is computed.\n\n=== ch5-q05 (correct=B) ===\nQ: An organization must be able to restore its data to a point no more than one hour before a failure. Which metric is being set?\n  [A] The recovery time allowed to bring the application back online.\n  [B] The recovery point to which data must be restored.\n  [C] The maximum tolerable downtime for the business function.\n  [D] The work recovery period before normal processing resumes.\nEXPL: The RPO is the maximum acceptable amount of data loss, measured as a point in time to which data must be restored, so a one-hour target is an RPO. The first option is the RTO, the third is the MTD, and the fourth is the WRT.\n\n=== ch5-q24 (correct=C) ===\nQ: Which BIA technique visualizes how processes, systems, people, and vendors depend on one another?\n  [A] BIA questionnaires sent across the business units.\n  [B] Criticality ranking matrices used to compare processes.\n  [C] Dependency mapping of processes, systems, and vendors.\n  [D] Impact-over-time analysis of how disruption harm grows.\nEXPL: Dependency mapping visualizes how processes, systems, people, and vendors depend on each other. Questionnaires collect consistent data across units, matrices compare processes for prioritization, and impact-over-time analysis shows how harm grows.\n\n=== ch6-q09 (correct=C) ===\nQ: Which of the following is NOT part of the training and testing cycle in the Preparation phase?\n  [A] Running employee awareness training on security topics.\n  [B] Performing tabletop drills and simulations with the IR team.\n  [C] Maintaining continuous real-time mirror copies of the data.\n  [D] Testing communication channels and reviewing the plan periodically.\nEXPL: The ongoing cycle includes employee awareness training, tabletop drills and simulations, testing communication channels, and periodic plan review. Creating real-time mirror copies is a backup operation, not a training or testing activity.\n\n=== ch6-q27 (correct=D) ===\nQ: Which of the following is one of the eight key items written into the running log?\n  [A] Salaries, expenses, and the total project budget.\n  [B] The response team's attendance and break times.\n  [C] Marketing materials used during the incident.\n  [D] Discovery, scope, and the backup that was used.\nEXPL: The eight key items are the incident ID, discovery, scope, severity, actions, notifications, evidence, and the backup used. Salary, attendance, and marketing details are not among the required items.\n\n\n--- ENC SAMPLE (2 per chapter) ---\n[ch1] Post-Audit Activities: The activities performed after the audit has been conducted to review, analyze, document, and communicate the results of the audit. Their core purpose is to help the organization understand the audit findings, address the identified issues, and determine appropriate actions for improvement.\n[ch1] Exit Interview: A meeting held at the conclusion of audit fieldwork, also called an exit or closing meeting, used to discuss audit findings, resolve questions or concerns, and address outstanding issues before the final audit report is released. It is held when necessary rather than being mandatory for every audit.\n[ch2] Security Logging: Security logs record activities occurring on computers, networks, applications, and security devices. Organizations use logs for four main reasons: to detect unauthorized access, investigate security incidents, verify security controls, and support audits and compliance. Logs answer four important questions: WHO performed the action, WHAT happened, WHEN it happened, and WHERE it originated.\n[ch2] Log Types and NIST IDPS Fields: Authentication logs record successful and failed logins, password changes, multi-factor authentication events, and account lockouts. Authorization logs record access to restricted files, privilege changes, and administrative actions. Network logs record firewall connections, blocked traffic, VPN activity, and network scans. System and application logs record operating-system errors, software installations, configuration changes, application failures, and user transactions. NIST identifies the timestamp, event type, event source, and sensor or reporting system as important log fields for IDPS events.\n[ch3] Exit Interview (Deep Dive): The final formal meeting conducted between the security audit team and key organizational stakeholders or management before the official audit report is published. It is an initial verbal debriefing that discusses the execution of the audit, initial observations, and preliminary findings, and represents the transition from field testing and evidence collection to formal reporting and remediation planning.\n[ch3] Exit Interview Format: A structured, high-level briefing held in person or via secure, recorded video conference, typically lasting 30 to 60 minutes and supported by an executive summary slide deck or position paper.\n[ch4] Risk Identification: The process of finding, recognizing, and documenting potential risks that may affect a project; it is the starting point of the risk management process and is guided by asking what could go wrong while developing the system.\n[ch4] Brainstorming: A risk identification technique where team members identify risks such as lack of resources, technical errors, or delays.\n[ch5] Business Continuity Management (BCM): An organization-wide framework of policies, processes, and capabilities that prepare a business to prevent, respond to, and recover from disruptive incidents.\n[ch5] Business Continuity Plan (BCP): The documented set of procedures an organization follows to keep critical functions running during, and immediately after, a disruption.\n[ch6] Full Backup: A backup type that copies all data. It is the slowest to create and uses the most storage, but restores the fastest because only one backup set is needed.\n[ch6] Incremental Backup: A backup type that copies data changed since the last backup of any kind (full or incremental). It is the fastest to create and uses the least storage, but restoring takes longer because multiple backup sets are needed.\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"& \"C:\\xampp\\php\\php.exe\" \"C:\\Users\\kelly\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\opencode\\ias2build\\spot.php\"","time":{"start":1789885939220,"end":1789885939532}},"id":"prt_0bd83fd4a001RM6YqPOzcObXNT","sessionID":"ses_f428ff92affeQ6wiY0s4pQPZG0","messageID":"msg_0bd83f3640017Tko3NVYhS8Gpx"}}
